    A captured frame over a kept static picture gives the scene's roots their state
    
    A frame described from the camera's own captures finds a drawn face's
    surface among the scene's roots, walking every root's family. Over a static
    picture that still holds, the frame gave only its moving mobjects their
    recorded state, so the walk read the other roots as the last drawing left
    them. Where the picture was kept by an earlier drawing (a session's next
    frame of the same play), that is the scene as it ended: a still surface
    that loses faces after the play was seen with its later faces.
    
    On a captured frame whose picture holds, the capture's draw list is shown
    with its moving mobjects, as a frame painted from the record and a static
    picture already are. Frames drawn on Cairo itself, which walk no roots,
    still show only what moves.
    
    (cherry picked from commit d1ae8d23833809ab510832a49ebf2a59167e525f)

